 "beats": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "They refused to load bombs, and faced mutiny.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "p4",
    "focus": [
     0.2,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.12
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.2,
     0.3
    ]
   },
   "section": "hook",
   "overlays": {
    "hook": "They refused to load bombs, *and faced mutiny.*"
   },
   "captions": false,
   "speed": 1.45
  },
  {
   "id": "name",
   "text": "This is Port Chicago, California, 1944.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Port Chicago",
    "sub": "California, 1944"
   },
   "section": "who"
  },
  {
   "id": "job",
   "text": "The Navy was segregated. Black sailors, with little training, were given the job of loading bombs onto ships.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "p6",
    "caption": "Loading ammunition at Port Chicago"
   },
   "section": "story"
  },
  {
   "id": "blast",
   "text": "On July 17, 1944, the pier exploded. Three hundred twenty people were killed. Two hundred two were Black sailors.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "p9",
    "caption": "Port Chicago after the explosion"
   },
   "section": "twist",
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1944"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "leave",
   "text": "White sailors got leave. Black sailors did not.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "No leave",
    "sub": "for the Black sailors"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "refuse",
   "text": "Three weeks later, the survivors were ordered to load ammunition again. Two hundred fifty-eight refused.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "258",
    "sub": "refused"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "fifty",
   "text": "Most went back. Fifty were tried for mutiny, in time of war, a crime that could carry the death penalty.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "recruits",
    "caption": "Black Navy recruits, 1940s"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "marshall",
   "text": "Thurgood Marshall came to watch the trial, and called them victims of negligence and racial injustice.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "marshall",
    "caption": "Thurgood Marshall"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "guilty",
   "text": "All fifty were found guilty, and sentenced to as much as fifteen years of hard labor.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Guilty",
    "sub": "all 50, October 1944"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "free",
   "text": "Most were released in 1946, and that year the Navy began to desegregate. But the convictions stood.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "1946",
    "sub": "the Navy begins to desegregate"
   },
   "section": "legacy"
  },
  {
   "id": "cleared",
   "text": "On July 17, 2024, eighty years to the day, the Navy finally exonerated them. None of the fifty was still alive.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "p11",
    "caption": "Port Chicago Naval Magazine National Memorial"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "end",
   "text": "They asked why. It took eighty years to answer.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "p6",
    "focus": [
     0.45,
     0.5
    ],
    "zoom": [
